elative to their mean and standard deviation, emphasizing deviations that might correlate with biodegradability characteristics.  \ndf['zscore_SM6_L'] = (df['SM6_L'] - df['SM6_L'].mean()) / (df['SM6_L'].std() + 1e-8)\n```end\n\n```python\n# sqrt_HyWi_B_mass  \n# Feature description: Square root transformation applied to the Hyper-Wiener-like index from Burden matrix weighted by mass (HyWi_B(m)).  \n# Usefulness: Reduces skew and makes hidden nonlinear relationships more evident, particularly for molecular weight-related indices.  \ndf['sqrt_HyWi_B(m)'] = np.sqrt(df['HyWi_B(m)'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Aggregation Features**  \n\n```python\n# Mean_C_N_topological_frequency  \n# Feature description: Average of C-N bond frequencies at topological distances 2, 3, and 4 (F02[C-N], F03[C-N], F04[C-N]).  \n# Usefulness: Aggregates the impact of C-N connectivity across various distances into a single feature, capturing its generalized influence on biodegradability.  \ndf['Mean_C_N_topological_frequency'] = df[['F02[C-N]', 'F03[C-N]', 'F04[C-N]']].mean(axis=1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Spectral_moment_difference  \n# Feature description: Difference between spectral moments of order 6 from Laplace (SM6_L) and Burden matrices weighted by mass (SM6_B(m)).  \n# Usefulness: Highlights structural differences as captured by spectral moments, which can be indicative of biodegradability potential.  \ndf['Spectral_moment_difference'] = df['SM6_L'] - df['SM6_B(m)']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Polarizability_per_heavy_atom  \n# Feature description: Polarizability summed (SpPosA_B(p)) and normalized by the number of heavy atoms (nHM).  \n# Usefulness: Captures average molecular polarizability per heavy atom, which influences chemical interactions and biodegradation.  \ndf['Polarizability_per_heavy_atom'] = df['SpPosA_B(p)'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-8)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Sparse or Redundant Features\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped  \ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)  \n# Sparsity: F01[N-N] has a very low mean (0.0474) and extreme sparsity (Q3 = 0), making it uninformative for classification.  \n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column nArCOOR is dropped  \ndf.drop(columns=['nArCOOR'], inplace=True)  \n# Sparsity: nArCOOR (mean = 0.0632) is mostly zero, contributing limited value to biodegradability prediction.  \n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped  \ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)  \n# Sparsity: N-073 (mean = 0.0363) is highly sparse and poorly correlated with biodegradability classification.  \n```end\n\n--- \n\n### Summary \nThese steps focus on generating meaningful interaction terms, reducing feature sparsity through aggregation, and applying transformations to normalize skewed distributions. These enhancements are designed to improve the signal-to-noise ratio for biodegradability classification while maintaining simplicity and interpretability.", "```python\n# Interaction feature: Oxygen-to-Carbon Ratio (O_C_ratio)\n# Feature description: Ratio of the number of oxygen atoms (nO) to the percentage of carbon atoms (C%).\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the balance between oxygen and carbon, which may influence biodegradability due to oxygen's role in oxidative processes.\ndf['O_C_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['C%'] + 1e-8)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Statistical feature: Mean Frequency of C-N Bonds (Mean_C-N_Freq)\n# Feature description: Mean of the frequencies of C-N bonds at topological distances 2, 3, and 4 (F02[C-N], F03[C-N], F04[C-N]).\n# Usefulness: Consolidates the importance of C-N bonds across various topological distances into a single feature, emphasizing the role of chemical bonding patterns in biodegradability.\ndf['Mean_C-N_Freq'] = df[['F02[C-N]', 'F03[C-N]', 'F04[C-N]']].mean(axis=1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Nonlinear transformation: Logarithm of dO E-states (log_SdO)\n# Feature description: Natural logarithm of the sum of dO E-states (SdO), with 1 added to avoid log(0).\n# Usefulness: Compresses the high range of SdO values, reducing skewness to help models better capture patterns in molecules with large dO E-states.\ndf['log_SdO'] = np.log(df['SdO'] + 1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column F01[N-N] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]'], inplace=True)\n# Reasoning: F01[N-N] has a high sparsity (mean = 0.0474, Q3 = 0) and is unlikely to provide significant information for classifying biodegradability.\n\n# Explanation why the column nArCOOR is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nArCOOR'], inplace=True)\n# Reasoning: nArCOOR is highly sparse (mean = 0.0632, majority = 0.0) and does not contribute significantly to the predictive task.\n\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n# Reasoning: N-073 has low variance and is predominantly zero (mean = 0.0363, all quartiles = 0), making it uninformative for the classification task.\n```end"]
